// ThreadServiceHandler is an implementation of the codefoundry.v1.ThreadService service.
type ThreadServiceHandler interface {
	// Create spawns claude in a worktree and returns the thread in STARTING state.
	Create(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.CreateThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.CreateThreadResponse], error)
	// Fork starts a new thread that continues an existing thread's conversation.
	Fork(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ForkThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ForkThreadResponse], error)
	// List returns all threads.
	List(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ListThreadsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ListThreadsResponse], error)
	// Get returns one thread.
	Get(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.GetThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.GetThreadResponse], error)
	// Wait blocks until a thread meets a condition (ThreadWaitCondition), returning at
	// once when it already does, or until the timeout (timed_out set, not an error). It
	// follows the thread list's changes, never polls. NOT_FOUND for an unknown thread or
	// one removed while waiting. The thread.wait command (docs/notes/agents-1-wait-events.md).
	Wait(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.WaitThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.WaitThreadResponse], error)
	// Rename sets a user-chosen name and disables auto-naming for the thread.
	Rename(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RenameThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RenameThreadResponse], error)
	// RegenerateName names the thread again with the naming model, from its most recent
	// turns (the last three user messages and the replies around them), replacing any
	// name, a hand-typed one too. It ignores threads.auto_name; threads.naming_model
	// applies. Synchronous: it returns the renamed thread once the model answered, bounded
	// by the naming timeout. While it runs the thread's naming flag is set. FAILED_PRECONDITION
	// for a resident thread, a thread with no user message yet, or one already being
	// renamed. See docs/notes/regenerate-thread-name.md.
	RegenerateName(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RegenerateNameRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RegenerateNameResponse], error)
	// Close ends the Claude process gracefully (interrupt, stdin closed, bounded wait,
	// then kill). The thread remains, DISCONNECTED, until Remove. No command calls it
	// since 2026-10-15: the daemon starts a thread's process when a message is sent
	// (Send) and stops it at the prompt cache expiry (docs/notes/thread-lifecycle.md).
	// Kept for compatibility.
	Close(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.CloseThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.CloseThreadResponse], error)
	// Reconnect resumes a DISCONNECTED thread in a new claude process, without a
	// prompt. No command calls it since 2026-10-15 (a message starts a stopped thread).
	// Kept for compatibility.
	Reconnect(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ReconnectThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ReconnectThreadResponse], error)
	// Remove forgets a thread. Stops its Claude first if it runs. Removing also removes,
	// the same way and first, every thread below it that is not shown in the sidebar
	// (Thread.in_sidebar), through hidden threads only, archived ones included: a shown
	// thread and everything below it stays (ThreadFilter.archive_cascade_of lists them,
	// with include_archived). A thread of those that fails is skipped, shown in the
	// sidebar and named in the error, which then comes after the thread itself was removed.
	Remove(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RemoveThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RemoveThreadResponse], error)
	// RunIn moves a thread's cwd to another of its checkouts (Checkouts): that member
	// becomes the primary. A live thread gets set_cwd once it is idle
	// (pending_worktree_path shows the queued target until then) and the row's
	// worktree_path and project_id change when Claude accepts it. A disconnected thread's
	// row changes at once; the next start resumes there. Refused for a thread without a
	// project and for a target that is not one of its checkouts.
	RunIn(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RunInThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RunInThreadResponse], error)
	// AddProject adds a project to a thread's set. Its checkout is placed by Create's rule:
	// in worktree mode a git project's worktree for the thread's branch (shared when one
	// is on it, else made; Create's refusals for a main checkout on the branch or a
	// default path holding another checkout), a plain folder its folder; in checkout mode
	// the project's own checkout. RemoveProject drops a member: refused for the cwd's
	// project; its checkout stays on disk. Either clears the thread's workspace_id (it no
	// longer follows that workspace; the workspace is untouched) and adds a timeline
	// note. A live Claude restarts, resuming the conversation, for its new --add-dir list
	// once it is idle at its prompt; a stopped thread takes it at its next start. Refused
	// for a thread without a project and for one running in its workspace's folder. See
	// docs/notes/thread-project-set.md.
	AddProject(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.AddThreadProjectRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.AddThreadProjectResponse], error)
	RemoveProject(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RemoveThreadProjectRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RemoveThreadProjectResponse], error)
	// Checkouts resolves a thread (by id, else the thread one of whose checkouts holds the
	// cwd, the deepest winning) and lists one checkout per project in its set: the primary
	// at its cwd; in worktree mode a git member's worktree on the thread's branch, a plain
	// folder the folder; otherwise each member's own checkout. See
	// docs/notes/workspaces-v2-1-threads.md.
	Checkouts(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ThreadCheckoutsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ThreadCheckoutsResponse], error)
	// CheckPlacement reports where a new thread with these projects, branch and mode would
	// go, per project, without making or changing anything: a new worktree at a path, an
	// existing checkout it would share, a plain folder, or the refusal Create would give
	// (with its message). It runs Create's own placement checks, so the two agree; like and
	// creator inheritance are not considered. See docs/notes/thread-info-facts.md.
	CheckPlacement(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.CheckPlacementRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.CheckPlacementResponse], error)
	// Pin pins or unpins a thread. Pinned threads sit in their own section at the top of
	// the GUI's thread list. Persisted; any state.
	Pin(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.PinThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.PinThreadResponse], error)
	// Archive archives or unarchives a thread. Archiving ends Claude gracefully like
	// Close (the thread is DISCONNECTED), kills and removes the thread's shells, unpins
	// it and records archived_at (kept when it is already archived). Unarchiving only
	// clears archived_at: the thread stays disconnected until a Reconnect. Reconnect,
	// Pin (pinned) and Send unarchive a thread implicitly. Persisted; any state.
	// Archiving also archives, the same way and first, every thread below it that is not
	// shown in the sidebar (Thread.in_sidebar), through hidden threads only: a shown
	// thread and everything below it stays (ThreadFilter.archive_cascade_of lists them).
	// A thread of those that fails is skipped and named in the error, which then comes
	// after the thread itself was archived. Unarchiving does not cascade.
	Archive(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ArchiveThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ArchiveThreadResponse], error)
	// SetSidebar shows or hides a thread another thread created in the GUI's sidebar
	// (Thread.in_sidebar), overriding the threads.show_created_threads setting for it:
	// shown hands it to the user, hidden back to its creator. INVALID_ARGUMENT for a
	// thread the user created (always shown). Persisted; any state.
	SetSidebar(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SetThreadSidebarRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SetThreadSidebarResponse], error)
	// SetWorkspace sets or clears a thread's workspace reference (Thread.workspace_id:
	// the workspace whose instructions and skills reach the thread); workspace is an id or
	// a name, "" clears it. The project set does not change. Persisted; any state; it
	// applies from the thread's next spawn (a live process keeps what it started with).
	// NOT_FOUND for an unknown workspace; FAILED_PRECONDITION to clear or change the
	// reference of a thread whose primary is its workspace's folder (empty project_id with
	// a workspace_id), whose cwd is that folder.
	SetWorkspace(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SetThreadWorkspaceRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SetThreadWorkspaceResponse], error)
	// Snooze snoozes a thread until a time, or unsnoozes it (until unset). Snoozing ends
	// Claude gracefully like Close (the thread is DISCONNECTED), kills and removes the
	// thread's shells, unpins and unarchives it and records snoozed_until (a re-snooze
	// replaces it). until must be in the future (INVALID_ARGUMENT otherwise). When the
	// time comes the daemon wakes the thread: snoozed_until is cleared and the thread,
	// still disconnected, becomes NEEDS_ATTENTION "snooze ended" (status_changed_at = the
	// wake time; a later Snooze or Archive turns it into IDLE "at prompt"). Unsnoozing
	// before then only clears snoozed_until. Reconnect, Pin
	// (pinned), Send and Archive (archived) clear a snooze implicitly. Persisted.
	Snooze(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SnoozeThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SnoozeThreadResponse], error)
	// Label adds and removes a thread's labels (clear first, then remove, then add) and
	// returns the thread. Any state. Persisted. See docs/notes/agents-2-metadata.md.
	Label(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.LabelThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.LabelThreadResponse], error)
	// Report records the thread's report (status, summary, JSON data), replacing the
	// previous one, stamped report_at. Any state. Persisted.
	Report(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ReportThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ReportThreadResponse], error)
	// SetBudget sets the thread's spending limit (Thread.budget_usd; 0 clears it). A
	// budget above the thread's cost re-arms the budget stop. Any state. Persisted.
	SetBudget(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SetThreadBudgetRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SetThreadBudgetResponse], error)
	// ClearFlags removes plugins' flags from a thread (Thread.flags): one plugin's, or
	// every one with plugin empty. NOT_FOUND when the thread has none to clear.
	ClearFlags(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ClearThreadFlagsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ClearThreadFlagsResponse], error)
	// Subscribe makes a wake subscription: when the target (a thread, or an action run)
	// meets the condition, the daemon sends the subscriber a message (a user_message item
	// with origin WAKE) through Send, which starts a stopped subscriber and unsnoozes it.
	// Edge-triggered, and once at once when the target already meets the condition.
	// FAILED_PRECONDITION for a subscription to itself or the reverse of an existing one.
	// See docs/notes/agents-4-wake.md.
	Subscribe(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SubscribeThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SubscribeThreadResponse], error)
	// Unsubscribe removes a subscription by id, or every subscription of a subscriber to a
	// target. NOT_FOUND when nothing matched.
	Unsubscribe(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.UnsubscribeThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.UnsubscribeThreadResponse], error)
	// ListSubscriptions lists one subscriber's subscriptions, or every thread's.
	ListSubscriptions(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ListThreadSubscriptionsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ListThreadSubscriptionsResponse], error)
	// Watch streams changes to the thread list (every thread's record, not its items).
	// The first event is a snapshot. WatchThread streams one thread's conversation.
	Watch(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.WatchThreadsRequest], *connect.ServerStream[v1.ThreadListEvent]) error
	// StageAttachment stores an image or PDF a message refers to and returns its path.
	// The file comes as bytes or as an absolute path the daemon reads (a file dropped on
	// or picked in the GUI). Create and Send append an "Attached image: <path>" or "Attached PDF: <path> (<N>
	// pages)" line per attachment and send its content as an image or document block; a
	// PDF over 10 pages goes only as its path, and Claude reads it with its Read tool.
	// Only image types and PDFs are accepted, with size limits (images 10 MiB, PDFs
	// 20 MiB, 24 MiB sent inline per message); unused staged files are reaped by the
	// daemon.
	StageAttachment(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.StageAttachmentRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.StageAttachmentResponse], error)
	// ReadAttachment returns a staged attachment's bytes (a user message shows its
	// images and PDFs). Only files directly in the attachments directory;
	// NOT_FOUND once the file was reaped.
	ReadAttachment(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ReadAttachmentRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ReadAttachmentResponse], error)
	// ListItems returns a thread's items (the streaming one included) and its pending
	// requests. Works while disconnected (items persist).
	ListItems(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ListThreadItemsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ListThreadItemsResponse], error)
	// ListPrompts lists the prompts the user sent in one thread, in the threads sharing a
	// project with it (or with a project set), or in every thread, newest first, from
	// their user_message items. Only the user's own: wake messages, messages another
	// thread sent (SendThreadMessageRequest.by_thread_id, a created thread's first prompt)
	// and attachment-only messages are left out, and so are threads a plugin owns and,
	// for PROJECT and ALL, hidden child threads. Archived threads count. See
	// docs/notes/prompt-history-1-daemon.md.
	ListPrompts(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ListPromptsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ListPromptsResponse], error)
	// WatchThread streams one thread: a snapshot first, then appended/updated items, text
	// deltas and pending-request changes. While a WatchThread is open the thread counts as
	// looked at: a finished turn is seen at once. Apply only
	// events whose seq is greater than the snapshot's.
	WatchThread(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.WatchThreadRequest], *connect.ServerStream[v1.ThreadEvent]) error
	// Send sends a user message. Allowed while a turn runs: Claude queues it or folds it
	// in. A thread that is not running starts with the message as its first prompt: the
	// returned item is in the timeline at once, and a start that fails is an error item
	// ("Claude did not start: <reason>", status ERROR "did not start"), not an RPC error.
	// The RPC returns at once with the item: a message to a starting thread is delivered
	// after its handshake, one to a closing thread once a new process started after the
	// old one exited. A message whose first non-blank line starts with a
	// slash command the app replaces (/model, /effort, /fast, /mcp, /rename, /clear, and
	// the aliases /reset, /new, /name) is INVALID_ARGUMENT, nothing sent or recorded; the
	// error's message is a sentence pointing at the app's control, shown as it is.
	Send(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SendThreadMessageRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SendThreadMessageResponse], error)
	// Respond answers a pending permission request or question. A thread that is not
	// running has none (FAILED_PRECONDITION).
	Respond(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RespondThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RespondThreadResponse], error)
	// Interrupt stops the running turn; pending requests are withdrawn. A no-op on a
	// thread that is not running.
	Interrupt(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.InterruptThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.InterruptThreadResponse], error)
	// SetModel switches a running thread's model (from its next request) and records it
	// for later spawns; on a thread that is not running it only records it.
	SetModel(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SetThreadModelRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SetThreadModelResponse], error)
	// SetPermissionMode switches a thread's permission mode: SUPERVISED, ACCEPT_EDITS or
	// AUTO (UNSPECIFIED, Claude's default, cannot be selected live). On a thread that is
	// not running it only records it.
	SetPermissionMode(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SetThreadPermissionModeRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SetThreadPermissionModeResponse], error)
	// SetEffort changes a thread's effort level. Claude has no live effort switch, so the
	// daemon restarts a running process with --resume and the new --effort: at once when
	// the thread is idle, else once the running turn ends (Thread.effort_pending says so
	// meanwhile). The row records the effort for later spawns either way; a thread that is
	// not running only records it.
	SetEffort(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SetThreadEffortRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SetThreadEffortResponse], error)
	// Restart restarts a running thread's Claude process with the same conversation
	// (--resume), the way an effort change does, so it picks up what changed outside the
	// conversation, such as a plugin's session environment (Thread.plugin_env_stale). A
	// running turn is interrupted first (a pending permission request or question with
	// it); the thread's background agents and shells stop with the old process. Messages
	// sent meanwhile go to the new process. A thread whose Claude is not running is left
	// as it is (restarted false): its next message starts Claude with the current
	// environment anyway. See docs/notes/plugins-env-restart.md.
	Restart(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.RestartThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.RestartThreadResponse], error)
	// Reseed gives a thread a fresh Claude session with a seed: its next process starts
	// a new conversation (no --resume) with the request's system prompt append and
	// add-dirs, which replace the thread's (empty clears them) and every later spawn uses,
	// and the first message of that session starts with prefix (UserMessageItem.seed_prefix).
	// The thread's items are kept; Thread.epoch goes up by one, context_tokens goes to 0.
	// A thread idle at its prompt with nothing running stops at once; a working one stops
	// once its turn and background work end (Thread.reseed_pending meanwhile); a stopped
	// one only records it. What "allow for session" granted dies with the old process, as
	// at any stop; the MCP status is kept until the new process reports. Any thread may be
	// reseeded; a resident thread is the main user. See docs/notes/ember-1-primitives.md.
	Reseed(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ReseedThreadRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ReseedThreadResponse], error)
	// StopShell stops one of the thread's own background shells (Thread.background_shells):
	// its process group gets SIGTERM, then SIGKILL 3 seconds later. A note in the thread's
	// conversation records it, and Claude learns of it as of any shell that ends. NOT_FOUND
	// when the thread runs no such shell; FAILED_PRECONDITION when its process cannot be
	// found. See docs/notes/thread-servers.md.
	StopShell(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.StopThreadShellRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.StopThreadShellResponse], error)
	// McpStatus returns the thread's MCP servers as last checked. The daemon asks Claude
	// (mcp_status) at the start of every turn, again every few seconds while a server is
	// still connecting, and after McpReconnect; WatchThread carries each new answer (the `mcp`
	// event). The last answer is kept while the thread is not running, also across daemon
	// restarts. refresh asks Claude now, starting a stopped thread without a prompt.
	// Without it a running thread not checked yet is asked once; status is unset when
	// Claude never reported. See docs/notes/mcp-panel.md.
	McpStatus(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.McpStatusRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.McpStatusResponse], error)
	// McpReconnect reconnects one MCP server of a thread, starting a stopped thread
	// without a prompt (also how a login made out of band, `claude /mcp` in a shell, is
	// picked up), then checks the servers again.
	// A server that still fails is not an RPC error: the response's error says why and the
	// status shows it. An unknown server is NOT_FOUND.
	McpReconnect(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.McpReconnectRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.McpReconnectResponse], error)
	// SlashCommands returns the slash commands a running thread offers (the composer's
	// "/" completion): what Claude reported in its initialize reply, replaced by each
	// list it pushes when skills change, minus the commands the daemon denies (replaced by
	// the app's own controls, terminal-only, or retired; never a skill or a user, project
	// or plugin command). In Claude's order: its skills, then its built-ins, then plugin
	// skills. What Claude last reported, kept while the thread is not running (also across
	// daemon restarts); empty when it never answered a handshake. WatchThread carries every change (the
	// `commands` event). See docs/notes/slash-commands-1-daemon.md.
	SlashCommands(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SlashCommandsRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SlashCommandsResponse], error)
	// ShowHtml shows an HTML page inline in a thread's conversation: an `html` item at the
	// thread's current position (inside the running turn when one is live, else after the
	// last item), which the GUI renders as a sandboxed frame (code-foundry view.inline;
	// docs/notes/inline-html.md). The file must be a regular .html or .htm file under the
	// home directory (InvalidArgument otherwise, NotFound when missing).
	ShowHtml(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ShowThreadHtmlRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ShowThreadHtmlResponse], error)
	// ShowTheme puts a theme card in a thread's conversation: a `theme` item at the thread's
	// current position, as ShowHtml places a page. code-foundry theme save adds one when it
	// runs from the thread (docs/notes/theme-model-flow.md). The theme must be a valid theme
	// file (InvalidArgument otherwise); nothing is written to the themes directory.
	ShowTheme(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ShowThreadThemeRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ShowThreadThemeResponse], error)
	// PageStorage returns what the thread's pages keep with the bridge's cf.storage: the
	// GUI host hands it to a page as it loads (docs/notes/bridge-fix-storage.md). Kept by
	// the daemon, one file per thread, because WebKit keeps a page frame's own
	// localStorage for the session only. NotFound for an unknown thread.
	PageStorage(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.PageStorageRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.PageStorageResponse], error)
	// SetPageStorage sets or removes one key of a thread's page storage and returns the
	// storage after. InvalidArgument for an empty or too long key (1 KiB), a value that is
	// not JSON, or a storage that would grow over 256 KiB (nothing is written then).
	SetPageStorage(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.SetPageStorageRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.SetPageStorageResponse], error)
	// ProxyFetch makes one HTTP request for a page of the thread (the bridge's cf.fetch,
	// docs/notes/bridge-fetch.md): the daemon is not subject to the browser's CORS check, so
	// a page reaches an API that does not allow the pages origin. No cookies, no Origin, no
	// Code Foundry token; up to 5 redirects of the same scheme; a request body of 1 MiB and a
	// response body of 8 MiB at most; at most 8 at once per thread (more wait). A non-2xx
	// status is a response, not an error. InvalidArgument for a bad URL, a scheme other than
	// http(s), a header the daemon does not send, a body too big or a destination it refuses
	// (loopback, link-local, unspecified, the daemon's own ports); DeadlineExceeded after
	// timeout_ms; ResourceExhausted for a response too big; Unavailable for a network error;
	// NotFound for an unknown thread. Cancelling the call aborts the request.
	ProxyFetch(context.Context, *connect.Request[v1.ProxyFetchRequest]) (*connect.Response[v1.ProxyFetchResponse], error)
}

